 "assumptions": {
  "a1": {
   "text": "About 38% of private employees lack paid leave usable for illness, so the term binds for them.",
   "class": "binding",
   "p": 0.9
  },
  "a2": {
   "text": "Most bound employers comply: payroll vendors build in accrual, and some workers complain.",
   "class": "enforcement",
   "p": 0.7
  },
  "a3": {
   "text": "Money wages are fixed in the short run by pay scales, annual raises and collective agreements.",
   "class": "other",
   "p": 0.8
  },
  "a5": {
   "text": "Wages above the minimum can adjust over the medium horizon, as raises are set.",
   "class": "other",
   "p": 0.8
  },
  "a6": {
   "text": "For the marginal worker and employer, the term is worth less to the worker than it costs the employer: they could have agreed on it and did not.",
   "class": "other",
   "p": 0.6
  },
  "a7": {
   "text": "The minimum wage binds for some uncovered workers.",
   "class": "binding",
   "p": 0.85
  },
  "a8": {
   "text": "Employers can move hours between part-time and full-time staff.",
   "class": "other",
   "p": 0.7
  },
  "a10": {
   "text": "Some firms near six employees value staying exempt more than an extra hire.",
   "class": "other",
   "p": 0.6
  },
  "a12": {
   "text": "Some existing PTO policies do not meet the K2 conditions (documentation, notice, increments, attendance points).",
   "class": "other",
   "p": 0.8
  },
  "a20": {
   "text": "The $3.4 million is new money from the general fund.",
   "class": "revenue_use",
   "p": 0.9
  },
  "a22": {
   "text": "Over the medium to long run, workers' alternatives to bound employment (small firms, contracting, Pellam) are clearly worse than employers' alternatives (automation, redeployment, Pellam).",
   "class": "mobility",
   "p": 0.6
  },
  "a25": {
   "text": "Owners of firms near six employees know from mid-2026 that the 2026 average decides their 2027 status.",
   "class": "expectations",
   "p": 0.7
  }
 }
}